<TopicPage slug="subjects/radiation-therapy/foundational/therapy-physics-and-the-linac">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Radiation therapy treats cancer by delivering lethal dose to a tumour while sparing the
    healthy tissue around it — a geometry-and-physics problem played out millimetre by
    millimetre. The core physics is almost paradoxical: ionising radiation damages every cell
    it passes through, tumour or healthy, so the cure comes not from a different chemistry but
    from <em>distribution</em> — stacking dose where the tumour is and spreading it thin
    everywhere else, then exploiting biology so the healthy cells recover better than the
    tumour does.
  </p>
  <p>
    The machine at the centre of that trick is the linear accelerator — the linac — a device
    the size of a small room that manufactures a precisely shaped beam of high-energy photons
    and swings it around the patient from any angle. Understanding the linac means understanding
    the trade's whole foundation: how the beam is made, how it behaves once it enters tissue
    (where dose peaks, how it falls, how it scatters), and how those behaviours are calculated
    into a treatment plan. Every number a radiation therapist reads on a treatment console —
    monitor units, energy, field size, depth — is an output of the physics on this page.
  </p>
  <p>
    One habit of mind is worth naming at the start: in radiation therapy, physics is a safety
    discipline as much as a curative one. The beam cannot be seen, felt or taken back, so the
    field's culture — redundant checks, dose calculations verified twice, positions verified
    daily — exists because physics without verification is dangerous physics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from microwave gun to fractionated dose" widget="equationStep" config={linacCfg} />

  <h2>Linear accelerator operation</h2>
  <p>
    A linac is a chain of specialists, each stage handing a more refined beam to the next. It
    begins with the <strong>electron gun</strong>, which injects electrons into a
    <strong>standing-wave accelerating waveguide</strong> — a copper tube in which microwave
    energy (nearly three billion cycles per second) sets up electric fields that surf the
    electrons forward, accelerating them to within a hair of light speed and to energies of
    roughly 6–20 megavolts (MV). A bending magnet then sweeps the beam around a corner —
    which doubles as a filter, because only electrons with the chosen energy can make the turn.
  </p>
  <p>
    What happens next defines the machine's two modes. For <strong>photon (X-ray)
    therapy</strong>, the fast electrons slam into a tungsten target, and the sudden
    deceleration converts their kinetic energy into a flood of high-energy X-ray photons
    (bremsstrahlung) that emerges roughly forward. For <strong>electron therapy</strong>, the
    target is retracted and the electron beam itself exits — useful for treating shallow
    targets like skin lesions and lymph nodes. The raw photon beam is forward-peaked and
    uneven, so a conical <strong>flattening filter</strong> (or, in modern
    flattening-filter-free designs, the beam's profile is accepted raw for higher dose rates)
    evens it across the field. Collimation follows: adjustable <strong>jaws</strong> set the
    rectangular field, and the <strong>multileaf collimator</strong> — up to 120 tungsten
    fingers, each up to a centimetre wide at the patient, driven individually — sculpts the
    beam to the tumour's cross-section, and moves live during treatment to paint dose
    intensity-modulation. The whole treatment head rides a <strong>gantry</strong> rotating a
    full circle around the couch, so beams converge on the tumour from any angle.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a linac stores radiation. Unlike a cobalt-60 unit, a linac
    is off when it is off — it generates photons only while powered, holding no radioactive
    material. That property shapes everything from machine servicing to decommissioning.
  </p>

  <h2>Dose calculation fundamentals</h2>
  <p>
    Dose is measured in the <strong>gray</strong> (Gy): one joule of absorbed energy per
    kilogram of tissue. Typical prescriptions — 60 Gy for a breast, 70 Gy for a prostate — are
    delivered in fractions of about 2 Gy, and the treatment machine tracks that dose through
    <strong>monitor units</strong> (MUs), a calibrated chamber in the machine's head that
    counts the beam actually delivered. Converting a prescription into monitor units is the
    discipline's signature calculation, and it multiplies a handful of measured beam factors.
  </p>
  <p>
    A simplified worked case shows the anatomy of the calculation. Suppose a plan prescribes
    2 Gy (200 cGy) per fraction to a tumour 8 cm deep, and the machine's calibration says one
    monitor unit delivers about 1 cGy at the depth of maximum dose (d<sub>max</sub>) for a
    10 × 10 cm field. The percentage depth–dose (PDD) at 8 cm for a 6 MV beam is about 70 per
    cent, so each MU deposits roughly 0.70 cGy at the tumour depth. The required monitor units
    are therefore 200 ÷ 0.70 ≈ 286 MU. Real plans add correction factors for field size,
    inverse-square distance effects, tissue inhomogeneities (lung transmits dose better than
    muscle; bone shields it) and — for modern techniques — the intensity map the leaves traced.
    The method is the point: every factor is measured, every multiplication is checked, and the
    final MU count is verified by a second person or an independent calculation before the beam
    is turned on.
  </p>
  <p>
    The three physical rules underneath every calculation are worth memorising as behaviors.
    Dose <strong>attenuates</strong> exponentially with depth as photons are absorbed and
    scattered. Dose falls with the <strong>inverse square</strong> of distance from the source
    (double the distance, quarter the dose rate) — the reason every 5 cm of patient geometry
    matters. And dose <strong>builds up</strong> below the surface before it decays — which is
    not a nuisance but the design feature the next section exploits.
  </p>

  <h2>Beam characteristics: the depth–dose curve</h2>
  <p>
    A megavoltage beam's dose versus depth — its percentage depth–dose curve — is the
    characteristic profile planners live inside. Dose starts low at the very surface, builds
    over the first millimetres to centimetres as secondary electrons scatter forward, peaks at
    d<sub>max</sub> (about 1.5 cm for 6 MV, about 2.5 cm for 10–18 MV), then attenuates slowly.
    The consequences are the trade's favourite facts. <strong>Skin sparing</strong>: the dose
    peak lies centimetres deep, so the skin itself is largely spared — a huge advantage over
    the orthovoltage era, and the reason bolus material must sometimes be placed deliberately
    when the skin <em>is</em> the target. <strong>Multi-beam stacking</strong>: because the
    beam attenuates slowly, a single beam delivers more dose to the entrance path than the
    tumour; aimed through the body from several angles, each beam's low-dose entry and exit
    paths spread dose everywhere, while the peaks and central beams overlap at the target —
    the geometric heart of conformal therapy.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="percentage depth–dose for a 6 MV beam" widget="plotter" config={pdCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>Energy choice</strong> then becomes a matching exercise: higher energies push
    d<sub>max</sub> deeper and attenuate less (better for deep tumours, worse for surface
    sparing of everything in between), while lower energies keep dose more contained near the
    entrance. <strong>Electron beams</strong> obey a different rule with its own neat
    arithmetic: their dose is deposited within a shallow, fairly sharp range — roughly
    E/3 centimetres of therapeutic depth for E megaelectronvolts — so a 9 MeV electron beam
    treats about 3 cm deep and stops, sparing what lies beneath. And <strong>field edges</strong>
    carry their own character: the penumbra, the zone of partial dose at the boundary, is
    narrowed by the collimator design and by placing the patient far from the source, and
    minimising it is what lets planners protect a spinal cord sitting millimetres from a target.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> radiation "burns through" the skin to reach the tumour, so
    skin damage is inevitable. The build-up region exists precisely to avoid this — surface
    dose problems in modern therapy are usually a sign of a deliberate choice (bolus, oblique
    beams) rather than an unavoidable cost.
  </p>

  <h2>From blocks to leaves: how field shaping evolved</h2>
  <p>
    The multileaf collimator's precision is recent history, and its evolution teaches how
    the physics earns its keep. Early therapy shaped fields by hand: <em>custom blocks</em> —
    dense alloy cast to the tumour's projected shape, wedged into the beam's path for each
    treatment — which worked but consumed hours per patient, allowed only one static shape
    per field, and exposed staff to daily handling. The first multileaf collimators
    automated the blocking with motorised tungsten leaves; modern ones go further, moving
    <em>during</em> the treatment so that dozens of changing field shapes deliver an
    intensity pattern no static block could paint. The clinical payoff is measurable:
    tumour concavities can be dosed while a nearby spinal cord or parotid gland is
    protected, and treatment times fell from tens of minutes of fiddling to minutes of
    automated delivery. The lesson generalises: in this discipline, every mechanical
    refinement of the beam is a way of buying normal tissue a smaller dose — and every
    fraction of a gray matters, because the doses that cure tumours are the same doses that
    hurt healthy tissue when misdelivered.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Fractionation is biology, not scheduling.</strong> Splitting the dose exploits a
    difference in repair: normal tissue repairs sublethal DNA damage between fractions better
    than tumours do, and tumours meanwhile reoxygenate (hypoxic cells are radiation-resistant,
    but dead oxygen-hungry neighbours expose survivors as the tumour shrinks) and reassort into
    more sensitive cell-cycle phases. Radiobiology's five Rs — repair, reassortment,
    repopulation, reoxygenation, intrinsic radiosensitivity — explain why the standard 2 Gy/day
    schedule works, and also why stereotactic regimes delivering huge doses (18–24 Gy) in one
    to five fractions required new technology — tighter positioning, steeper dose fall-off,
    image guidance — to stay safe: the fractionation biology changes when the dose per fraction
    does.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Modern delivery multiplies the physics.</strong> Intensity-modulated therapy
    (IMRT/VMAT) uses the moving leaves to vary dose rate across the field hundreds of times
    per beam, carving high dose around organs-at-risk with concavities no single field could
    produce. Image-guided radiation therapy (IGRT) adds daily on-board imaging — cone-beam CT
    or marker tracking — because tumours move with breathing and patients change day to day;
    sub-millimetre positioning is now routine. Flattening-filter-free beams trade beam
    uniformity for very high dose rates, which is what makes stereotactic treatments short
    enough for patients to hold still. Each layer is the same linac with a sharper question
    asked of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The team and the safety net are part of the physics.</strong> Radiation oncology is
    a triangle: the radiation oncologist prescribes, the medical physicist plans, calibrates
    and checks, and the radiation therapist delivers daily — positioning the patient to
    sub-centimetre accuracy and running the pre-treatment verification checklist. The
    profession's safety culture (independent MU verification, machine QA with daily, monthly
    and annual tolerances, incident-learning systems) exists because the beam cannot be seen,
    felt or taken back — and because history's radiotherapy accidents were, almost uniformly,
    physics errors that a verification layer would have caught. Learning the linac is learning
    both halves of that sentence.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Quality assurance gives the physics its daily numbers.</strong> Machine QA runs
    at three cadences: daily checks (a few minutes — output constancy, laser alignment,
    safety interlocks and door) before the first patient; monthly checks (a full set of
    output, energy and flatness measurements against tolerance); and annual
    comprehensive calibration by the physicist with a water-tank scanning system that maps
    the entire beam. Tolerances are tight by design — output, for instance, must stay
    within about 2 per cent of baseline, because a systematic delivery error repeated over
    thirty fractions compounds into a real dose error. The therapist's role in the same
    chain: the pre-treatment checks (interlocks, imaging-couch calibration), in-vivo
    dosimetry on selected patients, and the discipline of never treating on a machine
    whose morning check failed. QA is the least glamorous page of the physics chapter and
    the one the profession would name if asked which keeps patients safest.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ARRT Radiation Therapy Content Specifications; Khan, <em>The Physics of Radiation Therapy</em>; Washington &amp; Leaver, <em>Principles and Practice of Radiation Therapy</em>.</p>
